+# Full study: the Kanold diameter variant
+
+## Statement and hypotheses
+
+For a finite set `A` of points in the Euclidean plane, `DistancesSeparated A` says that whenever two distances determined by points of `A` differ, their absolute difference is at least one. The frozen claim asks for a positive real `C` and an eventual assertion over natural `n`: every such `A` of cardinality `n` has diameter at least `n^(3/4)`. The quantified `C` does not occur in the bound, but its positivity remains part of the exact source proposition and is not deleted.
+
+## Semantic binding
+
+The formal target does not restate `DistancesSeparated`, alter coercions, or substitute a broad import. It uses the source-qualified predicate and exact source module. The bidirectional transport is definitional: the target and source propositions have the same elaborated root in the pinned environment. Reverse coverage matters because a one-way implication could silently weaken hypotheses or outputs.
+
+## Composition and trust boundary
+
+The claim-owned proof root consumes the exact provider declaration through the checked transport. The worker records hashes and replay results, while canonical Master acceptance remains a separate trust boundary: Master must independently recompute expression and dependency evidence and is the only authority allowed to mark the theorem accepted.
+
+## Exceptional cases and downstream use
+
+Small cardinalities are covered by the eventual quantifier rather than erased. Empty or repeated-point edge cases remain governed by the original cardinality and finite-set hypotheses. Downstream Stage6 consumers receive the exact alias recorded in intake; no stronger open Erdos 100 variant is inferred.
